WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:03.510
Nicole: Let’s have a look at the
different ways that data can be stored.

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:08.130
Abraham: External Hard drives can be
used to store copies of important files.

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:12.759
The benefit for this is that they
are portable and sturdy and extend

00:00:12.759 --> 00:00:14.589
the storage capacity of a computer.

00:00:15.230 --> 00:00:18.750
They should always be encrypted
especially when sensitive data is stored.

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:22.680
Nicole: Cloud storage is becoming
more popular for safe storage of

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:27.340
files and can be accessible from any
device which has secured access to it.

00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:31.820
We all use cloud storage everyday as
many emails are stored in the cloud.

00:00:32.389 --> 00:00:36.970
Cloud storage means data is kept separate
from a user’s location and can be

00:00:36.970 --> 00:00:42.790
available 24/7 allowing organisations
extra storage and web services without

00:00:42.830 --> 00:00:45.440
investing in expensive hardware up front.

00:00:46.190 --> 00:00:50.600
It’s important to ensure that any cloud
storage has the right access controls

00:00:50.870 --> 00:00:54.420
because it’s so highly available
over the internet that only the right

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:56.290
people have permission to access it.

00:00:57.190 --> 00:01:01.629
Abraham: Flash drives are useful to keep
data to hand, but they are easy to lose.

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:05.339
Flash Drives should always be
encrypted, and password protected,

00:01:05.539 --> 00:01:07.690
especially when storing sensitive data.

00:01:08.530 --> 00:01:11.950
Nicole: Back ups are copies of
the system or network data for

00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:13.169
file restoration or archiving.

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:17.500
They are an essential part of a
‘business continuity’ plan for

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:19.040
data protection and recovery.

00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:24.179
Business critical data should always
be backed up regularly along with other

00:01:24.179 --> 00:01:26.619
key software components of a system.

00:01:26.889 --> 00:01:31.190
This not only stops opportunities
for blackmail by ransomware, it also

00:01:31.230 --> 00:01:35.100
protects from the impact of fire,
flood or physical damage or theft.

00:01:35.750 --> 00:01:40.160
Data backups should not be accessible
to staff or permanently connected to

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:44.499
the same network as the original over
a LAN to stop them being infected by

00:01:44.509 --> 00:01:48.469
Ransomware and other malware, should
that network become compromised.

00:01:49.059 --> 00:01:51.900
You wouldn’t want a breach to
take out both the live data

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:53.610
and your backups as well.

00:01:54.150 --> 00:01:56.500
It’s also important to remember
to ensure that your backups have

00:01:56.500 --> 00:02:01.229
the same or more stringent access
controls on as the live data.

00:02:02.860 --> 00:02:05.919
Abraham: Passwords are strings of
characters that verify the identity of

00:02:06.020 --> 00:02:08.210
users during the authentication process.

00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:12.230
Some people use the same password
all the time, which is not good!

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:16.619
This means that if one password is found
out, criminals will subsequently know

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:18.370
the passwords to all your accounts.

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:21.920
Some have too many and can't
remember them all so write them down!

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:23.019
Also, not good.

00:02:23.699 --> 00:02:27.140
Passwords need to be changed regularly
and should include different characters

00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:31.359
such as numbers, caps and lower-case
letters and other characters.

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:35.420
Password managers are a good way of
ensuring that you can have complex

00:02:35.490 --> 00:02:39.210
and unique passwords for every website
without having to remember them all.

00:02:39.740 --> 00:02:42.970
The longer the password is the
better as it is harder to crack.

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:47.020
Passwords can be made more secure
by processes like 'hashing' and

00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:50.799
'salting' which puts them through an
algorithm that turns plain text into

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:54.339
an unintelligible series of letters
and numbers so that if a password is

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:58.079
breached, the criminal is unable to
find out what your actual password is.

00:02:58.719 --> 00:03:02.130
Nicole: To prevent 'malware' from
affecting a system and to protect

00:03:02.130 --> 00:03:07.510
data, antivirus software also known
as anti-malware should be used and

00:03:07.550 --> 00:03:10.000
enabled on all computers and systems.

00:03:10.830 --> 00:03:14.160
These should always be kept up to
date to cover the latest threats.

00:03:15.099 --> 00:03:18.480
Abraham: Operating systems should
also be kept up to date and often

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:22.079
include antivirus software and
firewalls which create a 'buffer

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:25.720
zone' between a network and external
networks such as the internet.

00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:29.919
Operating systems should be set
to 'automatically update' and when

00:03:29.950 --> 00:03:33.300
updates are no longer available,
operating systems may need to be

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:37.390
changed to a newer, more current OS
or even a brand new computer system.

00:03:38.350 --> 00:03:41.960
Nicole: Share level permissions
manage access to folders shared over

00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:46.840
a network and don't apply to users
who log on locally, whereas file level

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:51.540
permissions grant access directly to
individuals’ objects such as files,

00:03:51.589 --> 00:03:54.019
folders, or hard disks on servers.

00:03:54.799 --> 00:04:00.000
Share permissions should be granted on a
role-based access control [RBAC] model.

00:04:00.590 --> 00:04:04.570
This means ensuring that people are
granted access to those shared files

00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:06.120
depending on what their role is.

00:04:06.670 --> 00:04:10.180
For example, only people in the
Finance Department should be

00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:13.890
able to access the shared space
containing the finance documents.

00:04:14.430 --> 00:04:18.110
Files within a given shared space
should also be further restricted.

00:04:18.470 --> 00:04:21.519
For example, within the finance
department, perhaps only the

00:04:21.529 --> 00:04:24.719
payroll people should have
access to salary information.

00:04:24.719 --> 00:04:25.055
Abraham: Encryption is a type
of cryptography where a message

00:04:25.055 --> 00:04:29.130
is encrypted so that only

00:04:29.809 --> 00:04:33.650
authorised parties can read it, converting
plain text into an unintelligible

00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:35.360
series of letters and numbers.

00:04:35.430 --> 00:04:40.129
Encryption is crucial in helping protect
against bad actors from around the world.

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:44.820
If all information was plain text
sensitive materials like passwords

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:48.170
and health records and bank
account information could be stolen

00:04:48.209 --> 00:04:49.489
and sold to the highest bidder.

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:53.920
Encryption works by granting a pair of
keys that allows only the holders of

00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:55.850
the keys to decrypt and read the data.

00:04:56.460 --> 00:05:00.350
It’s therefore incredibly important to
keep control over who has these keys.

